  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Triple Nexus approach in Somalia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project will be implemented in the Ainabo and Garowe districts of Sool and Nugal regions, reaching 200,000 people (60% women and youth to cover up to 25%). Oxfam will partner with Candlelight and KAALO, Oxfam&#x2019;s long-term local partners working on humanitarian, resilience-building and climate/environment programmes with presence in the target areas.

The target beneficiaries are pastoralist and agro-pastoralist communities affected by frequent droughts, necessitating a Humanitarian-Development-PeaceNexus intervention which not only addresses the immediate but also long-term needs, and offers alternatives for sustainable recovery and community-levelpeacebuilding. The proposed project will focus on providing support to the most vulnerable groups - Internally Displaced Persons and host communities. Beneficiary selection is based on an agreed vulnerability criteria i.e., women, children, the elderly, people with disabilities and youth. The selection of these specific groups is based on their increasedvulnerabilities especially during crisis. Oxfam seeks to address the vulnerability of women and girls through various empowerment initiatives, involving them in decision making platforms and strengthening their coping mechanisms and raising awareness of the protection risks at community level. People living with disabilities (PWD) are normally left out when decisions are being made at community level and as such, they become increasingly vulnerable as their needs are not always addressed during developmentinitiatives. A deliberate effort to involve PWD in the implementation of the project, empowering them to be part of the design and decision-making platforms will be the centre of the project.

During clan conflicts, youths and the elderly play a key role either in being agents and participants of the conflict as well as helping in resolving the conflict. By targeting them in this project, Oxfam seeks to provide options to youth that allow them to make informed decisions, strengthening their livelihood base, providing relevant training for conflict resolution. Elders as the key decision makers at community level remain key targets. Peace initiatives are hinged on the buy-in of these groups hence Oxfam will seek to strengthen their capacity through provision of conflict resolution training and providing a platform for discussion of key issues at community level.

Relevant Government line ministries and departments are also an important stakeholder and target for this project. Oxfam will aim at strengthening community resilience by creating relevant structures at community level that are linked with the relevant government departments. In this regard Oxfam will work to capacitate the ministries NADFOR  (National Disaster Preparedness and Food Reserve Authority, Somaliland) and HADMA (Humanitarian Affairs and Disaster Management Agency, Puntland) through strengthening of mobile early warning systems.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="24">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Candle Light for Health and Education (CLHE) works in the Toghdeer region of Somalia and was founded in 1995. Its mandate is to contribute to setting up sustainable structures in education and healthcare.
Its most important target group consists of women and other marginalised groups, such as returning refugees. CHLE is also working with former militia members and tries to integrate them in society.
CLHE works closely with the local authorities, leaders and others. Its work includes formal and informaleducation, training,preventive healthcare, rehabilitating small school buildings, and small-scale income generating activities. CLHE also works on the environment. It is of the opinion that deforestation has a negative impact on cattle, which has negative consequences for people&apos;s health and nutrition. Not enough good grazing land and a lack of water increase people&apos;s mobility, disrupting education programs.
Therelationship between Oxfam Novib and CLHE began in 1996. CLHE&apos;s organisation capacities havesince improved.</narrative>
